{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Thd1zineVv7wbneE1GFMfr","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"meta:\\\\n  last_updated: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  total_papers: 10\\\\ncategories:\\\\n  - key: \\\\\\\"contamination_detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    name: \\\\\\\"Benchmark Contamination Detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative: \\\\\\\"Methods for detecting training data contamination in LLM benchmarks, including RL-robust approaches\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - key: \\\\\\\"irt_equating\\\\\\\"\\\\n    name: \\\\\\\"IRT Methodology & Equating\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative: \\\\\\\"Item Response Theory models, calibration, equating, and their application to LLM evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - key: \\\\\\\"benchmark_design\\\\\\\"\\\\n    name: \\\\\\\"Dynamic Benchmark Design\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative: \\\\\\\"Approaches for creating contamination-resistant or self-updating benchmarks\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - key: \\\\\\\"dif_methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    name: \\\\\\\"DIF Statistical Methods\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative: \\\\\\\"Differential Item Functioning detection methods, power analysis, and small-sample strategies\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - key: \\\\\\\"item_generation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    name: \\\\\\\"Automated Item Generation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative: \\\\\\\"LLM-based test item generation with difficulty control and psychometric validation\\\\\\\"\\\\npapers:\\\\n  wang2026fragility:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"On The Fragility of Benchmark Contamination Detection in Reasoning Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Wang, Y.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02386\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"wang2026fragility\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"contamination_detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"foundation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Proves GRPO/PPO RL training conceals contamination signals (14-20pp AUROC degradation), motivating our DIF-based approach which operates on behavioral patterns immune to NLL smoothing\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  liang2026dvd:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DVD: Detection via Variance of Generation Distribution for Variant Contamination\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Liang, Y.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.04895\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"liang2026dvd\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"contamination_detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Detects variant contamination via generation variance (AUC 0.73-0.74 vs Min-K++ 0.43-0.68); complementary signal in our ensemble but untested under RL training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  tao2026selfcritique:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Detecting Data Contamination from Reinforcement Learning Post-training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Tao, S.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09259\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"tao2026selfcritique\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"contamination_detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Self-Critique/RL-MIA partially addresses RL concealment via policy-collapse detection, but remains individual-model-level; our DIF is group-level and complementary\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  polo2026growingpains:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Growing Pains: Extensible and Efficient LLM Benchmarking Via Fixed Parameter Calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Polo, F.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"polo2026growingpains\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"irt_equating\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Uses multidimensional IRT with fixed-parameter calibration and 100 anchor items for extensible evaluation (rho>=0.9); addresses score comparability but NOT contamination detection or item regeneration\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  polo2026psnirt:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Lost in Benchmarks? Rethinking Large Language Model Benchmarking with Item Response Theory\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Polo, F.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"AAAI 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"polo2026psnirt\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"irt_equating\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"PSN-IRT analyzes benchmark quality via 4PL IRT; provides published IRT parameters for MMLU we can use in semi-synthetic validation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  white2025livebench:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LiveBench: A Challenging, Contamination-Free LLM Benchmark\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"White, C.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"white2025livebench\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"benchmark_design\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Monthly-updated contamination-free benchmark with objective scoring; no difficulty calibration or psychometric equivalence across updates\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  zhang2024gsm1k:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Careful Examination of Large Language Model Performance on Grade School Arithmetic\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Zhang, H.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.00332\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"zhang2024gsm1k\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"contamination_detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"foundation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"GSM1K documents 10-13% average accuracy drops from contamination; provides ground-truth contamination evidence and item-level effect sizes (delta 0.8-1.4 logits) critical for our DIF power analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  hardy2026scalability:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Efficient Detection of Bad Benchmark Items with Novel Scalability Coefficients\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Hardy, M.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24999\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"hardy2026scalability\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"irt_equating\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Nonparametric signed isotonic R2 scalability coefficients outperform IRT item diagnostics; lightweight pre-filter for item quality before our parametric calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  kim2019fpc:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Application of IRT Fixed Parameter Calibration to Multiple-Group Test Data\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Kim, S.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Kolen, M.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2019\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Applied Measurement in Education\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"kim2019fpc\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"irt_equating\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Fixed-parameter calibration requires only N>=200 vs N>=500 for full calibration; core methodology for our bifactor IRT equating with anchor items\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n  belzak2020smallsample:\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Testing Differential Item Functioning in Small Samples\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Belzak, W.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2020\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Multivariate Behavioral Research\\\\\\\"\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bibtex_key: \\\\\\\"belzak2020smallsample\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"dif_methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Parsimonious logistic regression with sum scores outperforms complex IRT models for DIF detection in small samples; directly applicable to our N=50-80 model cohorts\\\\\\\"\\\\n    abstract: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    semantic_scholar_id: \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\n    verified: false\\\\n    added_by: \\\\\\\"director\\\\\\\"\\\\n    added_at: \\\\\\\"2026-05-20T17:30:00.000Z\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 7312, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011HKCLSuiqu55rkBMjCGk35","session_id":"570af1f9-c3cd-4db4-aa61-2d20e9ee2d29","uuid":"b75e53b4-ebfa-4d56-9ca7-fab04ae704c5","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:26:29.184Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"abd16142dcb53bef9","tool_use_id":"toolu_011HKCLSuiqu55rkBMjCGk35","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":26970,"tool_uses":8,"duration_ms":19391},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","uuid":"fb40d7d2-e205-4ee5-bcc7-95ab5271d0a5","session_id":"570af1f9-c3cd-4db4-aa61-2d20e9ee2d29"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0185YSVHZpZR3Yizbn27XqWE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AXGiXMus8VLfuP6f6eGvUy","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","file":"paper_framing.md","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7442,"cache_read_input_tokens":19111,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7442,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":48,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011HKCLSuiqu55rkBMjCGk35","session_id":"570af1f9-c3cd-4db4-aa61-2d20e9ee2d29","uuid":"86d81815-c15a-4899-a5c4-a82f1918a2f5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AXGiXMus8VLfuP6f6eGvUy","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/paper_framing.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Paper Framing — irt_equated_bench_regen\\\\n\\\\n## Target Venue\\\\n- Primary: ICLR 2027 (expected deadline ~October 2026; 5 months for full pipeline experiments)\\\\n- Backup: AAAI 2027 (abstract July 21, paper July 28, 2026 — tight, 2 months)\\\\n- Backup 2: EACL 2027 via ARR August 2026 cycle (August 3, 2026)\\\\n\\\\n## Core Claims (3 条, 可证伪)\\\\n\\\\n**Claim 1 (Detection)**: DIF analysis across model release cohorts detects benchmark contamination with practical power (ETS C: TPR 0.72-0.85, FPR <0.05 at N≥80, δ≥1.4) and is robust to RL training — because it operates on group-level behavioral response patterns (correct/incorrect conditional on ability), not within-model output distributions that GRPO/PPO smoothing affects.\\\\n\\\\n**Claim 2 (Ensemble Coverage)**: A multi-signal contamination sentinel combining DIF (large effects, RL-robust), ConStat-style perturbation testing (non-RL models), and DVD generation variance (variant contamination) achieves detection coverage across the full effect-size spectrum and model-type landscape, with each signal covering the blind spots of the others.\\\\n\\\\n**Claim 3 (Regeneration)**: Bifactor IRT equating with anchor items enables psychometrically equivalent benchmark regeneration — flagged items can be replaced with empirically calibrated alternatives that preserve construct validity, difficulty, and discrimination, maintaining benchmark comparability through a formal graceful-degradation framework.\\\\n\\\\n## Story Spine\\\\n\\\\n**Problem**: LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability — when we say \\\\\\\"Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,\\\\\\\" we presume both scores reflect the same latent ability. Contamination systematically erodes this validity. Critically, RL training (GRPO/PPO) conceals contamination signals: Wang et al. (ICLR 2026) show 14-20pp AUROC degradation for all reference-free detectors after ~156 GRPO steps, reducing most to near-random (~50% AUROC). The model ecosystem is shifting toward RL-trained reasoning models (o1, o3, DeepSeek-R1), making this a growing threat.\\\\n\\\\n**Existing approaches & gaps**: LiveBench updates monthly from recent sources but applies no psychometric calibration across updates. ConStat provides FDR-controlled performance-based detection but relies on perturbation sensitivity that RL can mask. DVD detects variant contamination via generation variance but is untested against RL. Growing Pains (arXiv 2026) uses multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation but addresses score comparability, not contamination. No existing work closes the full loop from RL-robust detection through construct-constrained generation to psychometrically equated regeneration.\\\\n\\\\n**Our framing**: Contamination is fundamentally a psychometric problem — it creates Differential Item Functioning (DIF), where contaminated items become differentially easier for exposed model cohorts beyond what general ability gains predict. DIF operates on group-level Item Characteristic Curves conditional on ability, making it orthogonal to within-model output distribution smoothing. This is the one contamination signal RL cannot conceal without negating the purpose of contamination itself: if a model memorized an item, it must answer it correctly, creating a detectable behavioral anomaly at the population level. Combined with a multi-signal ensemble for full effect-size coverage and bifactor IRT equating for calibrated item regeneration, this creates the first self-healing benchmark framework.\\\\n\\\\n**Headline result template**: \\\\\\\"On MMLU with 5,000+ models spanning 1.5 years of active LLM development (2023–mid 2024), DIF-based ETS C detection achieves 73-85% TPR with <5% FPR for large contamination effects (δ≥1.4), maintaining power where 10 existing detection methods degrade to near-random under RL training (Wang et al., ICLR 2026).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit Risks\\\\n\\\\n- **ICLR**: Strong fit — evaluation methodology, robustness to RL, psychometric theory applied to ML. Risk: reviewers may not be familiar with IRT/DIF methodology. Mitigation: provide self-contained DIF tutorial in appendix, reproducible code, and connection to ML-familiar concepts (DIF ≈ \\\\\\\"fairness testing for items\\\\\\\").\\\\n- **Cross-discipline bridge**: IRT/psychometrics vocabulary needs translation for ML audience. Key mappings: \\\\\\\"examinees\\\\\\\" → \\\\\\\"models\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"test items\\\\\\\" → \\\\\\\"benchmark questions\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"DIF\\\\\\\" → \\\\\\\"item-level contamination signal\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"equating\\\\\\\" → \\\\\\\"score comparability across benchmark versions\\\\\\\".\\\\n- **Novelty concern**: DIF itself is not new (1988). Our contribution is the novel application to LLM benchmark contamination + the RL-robustness argument + the integration with regeneration. Need to frame as \\\\\\\"bringing mature psychometric tools to solve an urgent ML problem\\\\\\\" rather than \\\\\\\"inventing new statistics.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\\\\n\\\\n1. **\\\\\\\"DIF power is only sufficient for large effects (δ≥1.4)\\\\\\\"**\\\\n   - Response: Large effects are precisely what threaten benchmark validity most (GSM1K: 10-13% average accuracy drop). Small effects (δ<0.6) have minimal impact on rankings. For medium effects, the ensemble's other signals provide coverage.\\\\n   - Evidence needed: plan_001 (real-data validation), plan_003 (ensemble)\\\\n\\\\n2. **\\\\\\\"Model cohort sample sizes are too small for classical DIF\\\\\\\"**\\\\n   - Response: (a) ETS C uses effect-size thresholds, not p-values, reducing sample-size dependence; (b) annual cohorts now reach N=80-100; (c) Cumulative Bayesian priors accumulate power over time; (d) Item-Focused Trees treat release date as continuous covariate.\\\\n   - Evidence needed: plan_001 (real cohort sizes), plan_008 (Bayesian DIF, P2)\\\\n\\\\n3. **\\\\\\\"Only validated in simulation\\\\\\\"**\\\\n   - Response: plan_001 uses METABENCH real data (5,055 models). plan_004 cross-validates on GSM8K with GSM1K ground truth.\\\\n   - Evidence needed: plan_001, plan_004\\\\n\\\\n4. **\\\\\\\"Item regeneration quality — how do you ensure replacements measure the same construct?\\\\\\\"**\\\\n   - Response: Bifactor IRT equating with anchor items + empirical calibration via student simulation (not prompt-based difficulty targeting). Construct validity verified through factor loading analysis.\\\\n   - Evidence needed: plan_005, plan_006\\\\n\\\\n5. **\\\\\\\"RL robustness is only theoretical — where's the empirical evidence?\\\\\\\"**\\\\n   - Response: plan_002 directly evaluates DIF on cohorts including RL-trained models.\\\\n   - Evidence needed: plan_002\\\\n\\\\n6. **\\\\\\\"Computational cost of the full pipeline\\\\\\\"**\\\\n   - Response: DIF analysis is lightweight (minutes, CPU only). Main cost is one-time calibration panel. Once calibrated, ongoing monitoring is cheap.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 6493, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011HKCLSuiqu55rkBMjCGk35","session_id":"570af1f9-c3cd-4db4-aa61-2d20e9ee2d29","uuid":"26060a76-ec9f-4270-bef6-3bbdb1495b3d","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:26:33.706Z"}
