{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012KeMRzYkDEE2kapQo6h3ef","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0141SXDf4hok9CaVJ3JVAT1x","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report — Consolidated Roundtable\n\n**Paper**: \"When Psychometric Contamination Detection Meets Heterogeneous Model Populations: A Negative-Result Study on DIF-Based Benchmark Monitoring\"\n\n**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers, 4-Step Roundtable)\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **R1 (Methods + Experiments)** | 3 / 6 (Weak Reject) — Confidence: High |\n| **R2 (Novelty + Related Work)** | 3 / 6 (Reject) — Confidence: High |\n| **Consolidated** | **3 / 6 (Reject)** — Confidence: High |\n\n**One-line verdict**: A technically competent but incomplete investigation that declares a psychometric approach unworkable without attempting the standard remedy (architecture-stratified IRT), while the core negative finding — that unidimensional IRT misfits a heterogeneous LLM population — is theoretically predictable.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThe paper investigates whether Differential Item Functioning (DIF), a classical psychometric technique, can serve as an RL-robust contamination signal for LLM benchmark evaluation. It tests this hypothesis on the METABENCH response matrix (5,055 models × 12,508 MMLU items) and cross-validates on GSM8K with GSM1K ground truth. The core finding is negative: the unidimensional IRT model severely misfits the heterogeneous model population (RMSEA = 0.11, CFI = 0.78), architecture differences generate massive spurious DIF (FPR = 0.12), and the hypothesized RL-robustness advantage is absent (DIF AUROC = 0.54 under RL vs. ConStat AUROC = 0.61). Additional experiments on ensemble fusion, bifactor IRT equating, item generation calibration, cumulative Bayesian DIF, and graceful degradation analysis all yield weak or negative results.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths (Consensus)\n\n1. **Comprehensive falsification across 10+ experiments.** The paper does not stop at one failure mode. It systematically tests the full proposed pipeline — detection → ensemble → equating → regeneration → cross-benchmark — and shows consistent negative results. This breadth rules out the possibility that a minor methodological tweak could salvage the approach.\n\n2. **Correct identification of the root cause.** Pinpointing unidimensional IRT misfit due to architecture heterogeneity (encoder-only vs. decoder-only vs. MoE) as the fundamental bottleneck is well-supported by standard psychometric diagnostics (RMSEA, CFI, TLI, SRMR, M² all FAIL). The diagnosis is concrete and actionable.\n\n3. **Ecologically valid experimental setup.** Using the full METABENCH dataset with 5,055 real models avoids selection bias. The temporal cohort design reflects realistic deployment.\n\n4. **Honest self-falsification.** The paper tests and falsifies its own core hypothesis (RL-robustness of DIF) rather than cherry-picking favorable subresults. The pivot from a positive-contribution framing to negative-result framing reflects intellectual integrity.\n\n5. **Practical engineering contribution.** The 7% maximum safe item replacement bound, while narrow, provides a concrete constraint that benchmark maintainers can use.\n\n6. **Appropriate psychometric methodology.** The use of ETS C classification (effect-size thresholds) over p-value-based DIF is well-motivated given the large item pool. IRT fit diagnostics are correctly applied and interpreted against standard thresholds.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Major (Both Reviewers Converge)\n\n**W1. Architecture-stratified DIF — the critical missing experiment.** Both reviewers independently identified this as the single most important gap. The paper declares DIF unworkable on heterogeneous LLM populations but never tests DIF within architecture-homogeneous subgroups (e.g., decoder-only models only, N ≈ 3,000–4,000 in METABENCH). If IRT fit improves substantially within strata (which is plausible), the correct conclusion is \"DIF requires architecture-homogeneous populations\" — not \"DIF doesn't work for LLM benchmarks.\" The paper cannot distinguish a fundamental limitation of DIF from a fixable violation of its assumptions. This omission is especially striking because the paper's own Discussion (§5.5) identifies \"architecture-stratified IRT\" as the path forward but never attempts it.\n\n**W2. The central negative result is foreseeable.** Unidimensional IRT on a population of 5,000+ LLMs spanning fundamentally different architectures trivially violates the unidimensionality assumption. Human examinees share cognitive architecture; LLMs do not. Any psychometrician would predict RMSEA >> 0.06 from first principles. The paper's core finding — that unidimensional IRT misfits a heterogeneous model population — confirms the obvious rather than revealing something surprising. The more informative experiment would have started with multidimensional IRT (MIRT) or the bifactor model as the primary analysis.\n\n**W3. The 12-model RL cohort renders RL-robustness conclusions unreliable.** This is the paper's most important experiment (testing the core RL-robustness hypothesis), yet it rests on only 12 identifiable RL-trained models. Bootstrap confidence intervals on AUROC with N = 12 are approximately ±0.15. The reported DIF AUROC = 0.54 vs. ConStat AUROC = 0.61 — a gap of 0.07 — is well within sampling noise. The paper draws directional conclusions (\"DIF is worse than ConStat under RL\") that the data cannot statistically support. No DeLong test, bootstrap CI, or permutation test is reported for any AUROC comparison.\n\n**W4. Related work is substantially inadequate.** At half a page, it falls well below standards for a paper bridging psychometrics and ML:\n- Missing: measurement invariance literature (Vandenberg & Lance 2000) — DIF is a special case\n- Missing: multidimensional IRT for NLP (Vania et al. 2021; Lalor et al. 2019)\n- Missing: test equating methodology (Kolen & Brennan 2014) — the definitive reference, absent despite equating being a core experiment\n- Missing: bifactor model literature (Reise et al. 2007; Rodriguez et al. 2016)\n- Missing: broader contamination literature (Jacovi et al. 2023; Golchin & Surdeanu 2024; Deng et al. 2024)\n- Insufficient engagement with Growing Pains (2026), which already does multi-group IRT for LLM evaluation\n\n### Moderate\n\n**W5. Ground truth quality insufficiently interrogated.** MMLU-CF's ϕ = 0.09 correlation with DIF flags is used as evidence that DIF fails, but the paper's own context notes that MMLU-CF has noisy contamination annotations. A low correlation is expected even under a functioning detector if the ground truth is unreliable. Without quantifying MMLU-CF's own reliability (inter-annotator agreement, coverage, FPR), the paper cannot distinguish \"DIF is broken\" from \"the ground truth is too noisy to validate anything.\"\n\n**W6. Negative-result framing overreaches its contributions.** The four claimed contributions are individually modest:\n1. IRT model fit characterization → confirms a known limitation\n2. 7% replacement bound → narrow, derived from one setting\n3. Correlated failure modes → descriptive, not actionable\n4. MMLU multidimensionality → already demonstrated by Growing Pains (2026)\n\nThe question is whether confirming that a method doesn't work, for theoretically predictable reasons, constitutes sufficient contribution for a top venue.\n\n**W7. Ensemble \"failure\" may reflect ground truth limitations.** The high signal correlations (r = 0.54–0.61) and 42% three-way overlap could arise because all three methods correctly flag the same truly-contaminated items. Without reliable ground truth, \"three correlated weak signals\" is indistinguishable from \"three signals that agree on easy cases because they're all correct.\"\n\n**W8. Graceful degradation analysis has circular dependency.** The 7% threshold depends on item calibration quality (ρ = 0.48 from Exp. 8) and equating quality (ρ = 0.85 from Exp. 7). No sensitivity analysis shows how the threshold changes with better calibration (e.g., ρ = 0.8). The paper establishes a single point estimate tied to other experiments' failures.\n\n### Minor\n\n**W9. 2PL model choice not justified over 3PL/4PL.** MMLU is multiple-choice (4 options, 25% guessing baseline). Omitting a guessing parameter is a non-trivial modeling choice that could affect DIF detection, especially for easy items where guessing inflates apparent performance.\n\n**W10. Cumulative Bayesian DIF underspecified.** No prior distribution, likelihood model, or formal definition of \"shrinkage factor = 0.45\" is provided. Insufficient detail for reproducibility.\n\n**W11. GSM8K experiment underpowered without power analysis.** N = 48, no post-hoc power analysis. At this sample size, Spearman correlation tests have ~80% power to detect ρ ≥ 0.40 only. The observed ρ = 0.11 could be \"no correlation\" or \"small real correlation masked by insufficient power.\"\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulations (e.g., decoder-only models only)? If IRT fit improves within strata, the paper's conclusion changes fundamentally.\n\n2. What are the bootstrap 95% CIs on all AUROC comparisons in Table 3? Can you rule out that the DIF-vs-ConStat gap is sampling noise at N = 12?\n\n3. What is MMLU-CF's own reliability (inter-annotator agreement, coverage of true contamination)? Without this, ϕ = 0.09 is uninterpretable.\n\n4. Why was 2PL chosen over 3PL/4PL, given MMLU's multiple-choice format?\n\n5. For the bifactor model, what percentage of the 26.5% non-convergent items are Heywood cases vs. slow convergence? Could regularized estimation improve convergence?\n\n6. Did you test whether RL models show systematically different discrimination parameters (a-values) than SFT models? This would directly test the ICC-shape-change hypothesis.\n\n7. What is the replacement threshold if you assume best-case calibration quality (ρ = 0.80 instead of 0.48)?\n\n8. Did you try ensemble methods beyond simple voting and weighted fusion (e.g., stacking, learned combination)?\n\n---\n\n## Missing Experiments (Priority Order)\n\n1. **Architecture-stratified DIF analysis** — Run DIF within decoder-only models only (N ≈ 3,000+). Report IRT fit diagnostics and DIF detection performance. *This is the single most important missing experiment.*\n\n2. **Semi-synthetic validation** — Inject known contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix and measure DIF detection power. Provides controlled ground truth that MMLU-CF and GSM1K cannot.\n\n3. **Statistical tests on AUROC comparisons** — DeLong test or bootstrap comparison for DIF vs. ConStat under RL. Currently all comparisons are point estimates without uncertainty quantification.\n\n4. **IRT fit diagnostics for architecture-homogeneous subgroups** — RMSEA, CFI for decoder-only models. If fit improves to acceptable ranges, the paper's conclusion changes.\n\n5. **Sensitivity of graceful degradation to calibration quality** — Parameterize replacement threshold by calibration ρ (0.4 to 0.9).\n\n6. **Contamination prevalence estimation** — The paper never estimates the contamination base rate, which is necessary to compute precision from the reported TPR/FPR.\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Convergence Summary\n\n### Points of Full Agreement (R1 + R2)\n- Architecture-stratified DIF is the critical missing experiment (both identified independently)\n- The negative result is predictable from first principles\n- The 12-model RL cohort is inadequate for the paper's central claim\n- Related work needs substantial expansion\n- Ground truth quality is a confound that weakens all validation experiments\n- The paper stops one experiment short of its most important result\n\n### Points of Partial Disagreement\n- **Related work severity**: R2 rates it as major; R1 considers it moderate (some references are \"nice to have\" for an ML venue)\n- **Overall score justification**: R2 leans Reject (the negative result adds too little); R1 leans Weak Reject (the execution quality and 7% bound have some value). Both land at 3.\n\n### What Would Change the Score\nBoth reviewers agree the paper could reach **5 (Weak Accept)** with:\n1. Architecture-stratified DIF results (positive or negative — either is informative)\n2. Bootstrap CIs and statistical tests on RL-robustness AUROC comparisons\n3. Expanded related work engaging with measurement invariance, MIRT, and Growing Pains\n4. Justification for 2PL over 3PL/4PL\n5. Sensitivity analysis on graceful degradation bounds\n\n---\n\n## Final Consolidated Score\n\n**Score: 3 / 6 (Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: High**\n\n**Justification**: The paper tackles an important problem (RL-robust contamination detection) with commendable experimental breadth (10+ experiments) and honest self-falsification. However, it suffers from three fatal gaps. First, the core negative finding (unidimensional IRT misfits heterogeneous LLM populations) is theoretically foreseeable and does not generate sufficient new insight. Second, the paper never attempts the obvious standard remedy — architecture-stratified DIF — before declaring the approach unworkable, leaving its conclusion as \"incomplete investigation\" rather than \"convincing negative result.\" Third, the central RL-robustness experiment rests on only 12 models without statistical uncertainty quantification, making the claim that \"DIF is not RL-robust\" as unreliable as the original claim it seeks to refute. As a negative-result paper, the bar is to convincingly establish that the negative result is robust; here, multiple plausible confounds (architecture mixing, noisy ground truth, underpowered RL comparison) leave the conclusion merely inconclusive.\n","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"worst","dream_id":"20260520T194223","overall_score":"3/6 Reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":950,"cache_read_input_tokens":73505,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":950},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"570af1f9-c3cd-4db4-aa61-2d20e9ee2d29","uuid":"0777603f-053c-42e4-9f6d-e28eabdf7a10"}
